  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["U2", "SW1", "Module1"],
      "pins": ["U2.4", "U2.2", "SW1.1", "SW1.2", "Module1.99"],
      "nets": ["GLOBAL_EN", "RUN_PG"],
      "observed": "On the CM4 sheet, U2 (a single buffer with VCC=+5V, GND=GND, /OE driven by the CM4 RUN_PG signal) has its output Y wired to SW1 pin 1, and SW1 pin 2 is the GLOBAL_EN net that goes to Module1 pin 99 (Global_EN). Thus the CM4 power-enable line is gated through an active buffer (U2) whose enable is RUN_PG, rather than being tied straight to a rail through the switch.",
      "expected": "The specification states GLOBAL_EN is 'driven by a simple switch with no sequencing component in between' and that 'the netlist confirms there is currently no delay/sequencing IC on this net, only a direct switch connection.' The intended design has SW1 connecting GLOBAL_EN directly, with no buffer/sequencing gate (such as U2 driven by RUN_PG) in the path.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "CM4 power-enable signal, driven by a simple switch with no sequencing component in between ... the netlist confirms there is currently no delay/sequencing IC on this net, only a direct switch connection."},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "CM4 sheet: U2 /OE = RUN_PG, VCC = +5V, Y -> SW1 pin 1; SW1 pin 2 -> GLOBAL_EN -> Module1 pin 99 (Global_EN)."}
      ],
      "impact": "The enable net now depends on RUN_PG through U2 instead of a direct switch-to-rail connection. Because RUN_PG is the CM4's own power-good output, gating GLOBAL_EN on it is circular at power-on, so the board's power-enable sequencing differs from the intended design and the CM4 may fail to enable/boot as specified.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build adds a sequencing buffer (U2, enabled by RUN_PG) between SW1 and the CM4 GLOBAL_EN enable net, which directly contradicts the specification's statement that GLOBAL_EN is driven by a simple switch with no sequencing component in between; this is the one deviation the drawing supports against the documented intended design."
}

